{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: roles, flow and instances for hosted agent teams. Write notes/2026-10-21-roles-flow-and-instances.md, and in the same head amend notes/2026-10-15-hosted-agent-teams-design.md (sections 2, 4 and 5, and the Counter demo) to cite it. Planner: manage this to completion. Write the note in a planner worktree, file a checker review, land when approved, then revise the Counter contracts (revision 4) to the instance model and file the builder slices in the order the note gives.\n\nBackground. Hugh, 2026-10-21, after reading Josh Rosen's \"The Software Factory Debate: Build, Buy, Fork or Delegate\" (x.com/JoshARosen/status/2112902388302467493). Rosen judges a software factory on four jobs: getting work in, coordinating it, verifying it, improving it. He rejects the delegate option (an always-on agent coordinating itself) because there is no single system the work passes through, so evidence scatters and nothing can be measured; he proposes checkpoints, a statement of what the work must satisfy and what evidence proves it. dap's journal is that single system, and dap-room's lanes are checkpoint definitions with authority. Of the levers that would make dap the attractive layer in that market, Hugh chose two to act on now: (2) a hosted agent team whose every decision is an act in the record, and (3) connectors as participants (a separate request). He extends (2): roles drive behaviour, with planner, builder and checker as the three that work well with distinct skills and a coordination flow; and a team of agents must accommodate multiple instances of the same agent identity explicitly.\n\nWhat exists (main 603220b9).\n- Membership (packages/platform/src/membership.ts): member kinds person, agent and checker; roster roles admin, maintainer, member, agent and checker, each an action list. An agent member names an active person as controller; a checker names none. invite-key opens a key of kind device, agent or checker for an active member, and enrol is signed by the new key, so one member may hold several active keys. revoke-key retires one key and leaves the member. The CLI enrols a further agent key from an agent-key link (enrolAgentKey in packages/cli/src/invitations.ts).\n- Every entry retains the observation it was judged under: member and key, role and actions, controller and whether the controller was active (packages/contract/src/observation.ts). The record already tells identity from instance.\n- Lanes (apps/room/lanes/src/issue.ts): take-hold is refused hold-held when a hold is already held under the commitment; the performer is the signer's member. change.review and change.merge are person-only by default; an author cannot review their own change; a controller's approval of a controlled agent's change counts only when the rules set ownerMayReview.\n- The hosted agent teams design (notes/2026-10-15-hosted-agent-teams-design.md, revision 2, delivered under 4b236bb2 and 2b90849c) separates role revision, team configuration, agent, assignment, execution instance, workspace and operation. It says roles choose behaviour and the app grants authority, that a planner, builder or reviewer role is not a membership permission, and that handoffs between agents are app records, never runtime messages. It models a desired count of three as three agents, each a member with its own key, with one active assignment per agent.\n- The room docs say builder, planner and reviewer are jobs, not roles (apps/room/docs/roles-and-membership.md).\n- Our own practice: this workroom runs on the planner, builder and checker identities. Several sessions act as planner at once (interactive and headless surveys) and several as builder. The collisions are recorded as lessons: check for landing heads before landing, verify review request IDs, one lane per head. Instances of one identity are already real here and unmodelled.\n\nDirection (Hugh, 2026-10-21, with the planner's proposal agreed).\n1. A role is three things together. Authority: the roster role it needs, its kind (agent or checker) and its controller. Behaviour: a role revision, with model, prompt and skills. Flow: its place in the coordination flow, the lane acts it watches for and performs. Planner: file issues with conditions, make plans, accept reports, review requests, ratify and land. Builder: commit, take the hold, report, propose the change on an exact base. Checker: review the exact version and submit check results; the checker kind, with no controller, is recorded independence. Handoffs between roles are app records.\n2. Multiple instances of one identity. Same mandate, same member, more keys; different mandate, different member. A slot in a team configuration is a key of one member, not a member. Desired count is the number of active keys of that member. One active assignment per key. Drain means retire a key. Presence and the team view show instances under the identity. Instances of one member never count as independent review, and separate members under one controller do not either.\n3. The Counter is the first test of the instance model: one identity with three instances taking turns, before any Room work.\n\nThe note must settle:\n- The role preset as data: fields for authority (roster role, kind, controller), behaviour (a role revision reference), flow (a pickup filter over lane acts and item states, and the acts it performs) and skills; and whether a preset belongs to an application package (notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md) or to host configuration.\n- The three presets planner, builder and checker written out against the issue and change lanes: the acts, the handoff points, and where a person must decide (plan 032's one counted approval).\n- The instance model: slot is a key; the host enrols the Nth key by invite-key and enrol under the controller's authority; an assignment is claimed per key; what a second instance of the same member reads when the first holds the commitment (the losing contender rereads, section 5 of the agent-teams note); drain and stop by revoke-key; recovery when a key is lost mid-assignment; what the record shows (member and key on every entry today) and what the views show.\n- Which acceptance cases of the agent-teams note change: racing two agents for one assignment becomes racing two instances; revoking an agent becomes retiring one key as distinct from removing the member.\n- The Counter demo reframed: one member with N keys; the commitments definition's limit of 16 participants counts members, so instances do not consume it; the changes to the Counter contracts for revision 4.\n- What does not change: authority comes from membership; the app ceiling and role budgets are shared by a member's instances (say whether per-key sub-limits exist); a reviewer prompt does not establish independence.\n- Open questions to answer or name: whether any lane guard needs to see the key (H2 reads role and controller; is there a rule that needs \"not the same key\"?); whether a hold should be per key (today per commitment, performer the member); presence per key in the member and presence UI (note landed at 9554919b).\n\nAcceptance. The note states the role preset shape and the three presets with their flow against the current lanes; states the instance rule and the slot-is-a-key model with enrolment, claim, drain, stop and recovery; lists the amendments to the agent-teams note and to the Counter contracts; names the Counter instance demo as the first exit test and the Room team as the second; and orders the builder slices. Document only; checker review before landing. Style: STE, scene first, terms in order, no registry, decisions or open points in the body (spec writing preferences). Public material: no product names other than GitHub and no comparisons.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no comparisons; no product names but GitHub; implementation requests follow after the note lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}